nss-drv: bound the firmware-supplied port range in the edma stats sync

nss_edma_stats.c takes both loop bounds straight from the message:

	for (i = nepss->start_port; i < nepss->end_port; i++)
		edma_stats.port[i]... = nepss->port_stats[j]...;

port_stats[] is a flexible array, so j walks off the response buffer
while i walks off port[NSS_EDMA_NUM_PORTS_MAX]. The header already states
the intended subset size, NSS_EDMA_STATS_MSG_MAX_PORTS, and nothing in
the tree referenced it. Reject a range that is empty, ends past the array
or is wider than one message can carry - a bad pair means the message is
junk, so there is nothing to salvage by clamping.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

--- a/nss_edma_stats.c
+++ b/nss_edma_stats.c
@@ -751,6 +751,19 @@ void nss_edma_metadata_port_stats_sync(s
 	uint16_t i, j = 0;
 	struct nss_top_instance *nss_top = nss_ctx->nss_top;
 
+	/*
+	 * Both bounds are firmware-written, and port_stats[] is a flexible
+	 * array: i walks edma_stats.port[NSS_EDMA_NUM_PORTS_MAX] while j walks
+	 * off the message. NSS_EDMA_STATS_MSG_MAX_PORTS is the subset size the
+	 * payload was sized for, which nothing in the tree enforced.
+	 */
+	if (nepss->start_port >= nepss->end_port ||
+			nepss->end_port > NSS_EDMA_NUM_PORTS_MAX ||
+			nepss->end_port - nepss->start_port > NSS_EDMA_STATS_MSG_MAX_PORTS) {
+		nss_warning("%px: bad port stats range %u..%u", nss_ctx, nepss->start_port, nepss->end_port);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	spin_lock_bh(&nss_top->stats_lock);
 
 	/*
